    "id": "ch1-intro",
    "title": "What is JavaScript?",
    "minutes": 3,
    "md": "JavaScript is a high-level, interpreted programming language\n\nKey Characteristics\n\n| Feature | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| Client-side scripting | Runs in the user's browser, not on the server |\n| Interpreted | Executes line-by-line without prior compilation |\n| Dynamic typing | Variables can hold any data type without explicit declaration |\n| Event-driven | Responds to user actions like clicks, keypresses, and mouse movements |\n| Prototype-based | Uses prototypes for inheritance rather than classes (though classes are now supported) |\n| Single-threaded | Executes one operation at a time with an event loop for async operations |\n\nWhat JavaScript Can Do\n- **Manipulate HTML and CSS** — Update content, styles, and structure of web pages dynamically\n- **Handle user interactions** — Respond to clicks, form submissions, scroll events, etc.\n- **Make API calls** — Fetch data from servers without reloading the page (AJAX)\n- **Validate forms** — Check user input before submission\n- **Create animations** — Produce smooth visual effects and transitions\n- **Build games** — Develop `browser-based` games using Canvas or WebGL\n- **Work with graphics** — Draw 2D and 3D graphics\n- **Store data locally** — Use localStorage, sessionStorage, or IndexedDB\n- **Control multimedia** — Play, pause, and manipulate `audio/video`\n",
    "raw": "JavaScript is a high-level, interpreted programming language\n\nKey Characteristics\n\n    Feature\t                                Description\nClient-side scripting\t        Runs in the user's browser, not on the server\nInterpreted\t                    Executes line-by-line without prior compilation\nDynamic typing\t                Variables can hold any data type without explicit declaration\nEvent-driven\t                Responds to user actions like clicks, keypresses, and mouse movements\nPrototype-based\t                Uses prototypes for inheritance rather than classes (though classes are now supported)\nSingle-threaded\t                Executes one operation at a time with an event loop for async operations\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhat JavaScript Can Do\n\n    Manipulate HTML and CSS – Update content, styles, and structure of web pages dynamically\n\n    Handle user interactions – Respond to clicks, form submissions, scroll events, etc.\n\n    Make API calls – Fetch data from servers without reloading the page (AJAX)\n\n    Validate forms – Check user input before submission\n\n    Create animations – Produce smooth visual effects and transitions\n\n    Build games – Develop browser-based games using Canvas or WebGL\n\n    Work with graphics – Draw 2D and 3D graphics\n\n    Store data locally – Use localStorage, sessionStorage, or IndexedDB\n\n    Control multimedia – Play, pause, and manipulate audio/video"
