  "ch2-scopes": {
    "id": "ch2-scopes",
    "title": "Scopes",
    "minutes": 4,
    "md": "global scope : variables declared outside of any function are in the global scope and can be accessed from anywhere in the code.\n\n```js\nvar globalVar = \"I am a global variable\";\nfunction example() {\n    console.log(globalVar); // Output: I am a global variable\n}\nexample();\n```\n\n---\n\nEND\n\n---\n\nlocal scope : variables declared within a function are in the local scope and cannot be accessed outside of that function.\n\n```js\nfunction myFunction() {\n    var localVar = \"I am a local variable\";\n    console.log(localVar); // Output: I am a local variable\n}\nmyFunction();\n```\n\n```js\nconsole.log(localVar); // Uncaught ReferenceError: localVar is not defined\n```\n\n---\n\nEND\n\n---\n\nfunction scope : variables declared within a function are in the function scope and cannot be accessed outside of that function.\n\n```js\nfunction myFunction() {\n    var functionVar = \"I am a function variable\";\n    console.log(functionVar); // Output: I am a function variable\n}\nmyFunction();\n```\n\n```js\nconsole.log(functionVar); // Uncaught ReferenceError: functionVar is not defined\n```\n\nblock scope : variables declared with let or const within a block (e.g., inside an if statement or a loop) are in the block scope and cannot be accessed outside of that block.\n\n```js\nif (true) {\n    let blockVar = \"I am a block variable\";\n    console.log(blockVar); // Output: I am a block variable\n}\n```\n\n```js\nconsole.log(blockVar); // Uncaught ReferenceError: blockVar is not defined\n```\n\n---\n\nEND\n\n---\n\nscope chain : when a variable is accessed, JavaScript looks for it in the current scope. If it doesn't find it, it looks in the parent scope, and so on, until it reaches the global scope. This is known as the scope chain.\n\n```js\nfunction outer() {\n    let outerVar = \"I am an outer variable\";\n    function inner() {\n        console.log(outerVar); // Output: I am an outer variable\n    }\n    inner();\n}\nouter();\n```\n\n---\n\nEND\n\n---\n\nclosures : a closure is a function that retains access to its outer function's variables even after the outer function has finished executing. This allows the inner function to \"remember\" the environment in which it was created.\n\n```js\nfunction makeCounter() {\n    let count = 0;\n    return function() {\n        count++;\n        console.log(count);\n    }\n}\nconst counter = makeCounter();\ncounter(); // Output: 1\ncounter(); // Output: 2\ncounter(); // Output: 3\n```\n\n---\n\nEND\n\n---\n\nlexical scope : a function can access variables from its parent scope, even if the parent function has finished executing. This is because of closures, which allow inner functions to retain access to their outer function's variables.\n\n```js\nlet x = 10;\nfunction foo() {\n    let x = 20;\n    function bar() {\n        console.log(x); // What does this print?\n    }\n    return bar;\n}\nconst baz = foo();\nbaz(); // Output: 20 (because bar lexically scoped to foo)\n```\n",
    "raw": "// global scope : variables declared outside of any function are in the global scope and can be accessed from anywhere in the code.\n\nvar globalVar = \"I am a global variable\";\nfunction example() {\n    console.log(globalVar); // Output: I am a global variable\n}\nexample();\n\n// =================================================================\n//                              END\n// ================================================================\n\n\n// local scope : variables declared within a function are in the local scope and cannot be accessed outside of that function.\nfunction myFunction() {\n    var localVar = \"I am a local variable\";\n    console.log(localVar); // Output: I am a local variable\n}\nmyFunction();\n// console.log(localVar); // Uncaught ReferenceError: localVar is not defined\n// =================================================================\n//                              END\n// ================================================================\n\n// function scope : variables declared within a function are in the function scope and cannot be accessed outside of that function.\n\nfunction myFunction() {\n    var functionVar = \"I am a function variable\";\n    console.log(functionVar); // Output: I am a function variable\n}\nmyFunction();\n// console.log(functionVar); // Uncaught ReferenceError: functionVar is not defined\n\n// block scope : variables declared with let or const within a block (e.g., inside an if statement or a loop) are in the block scope and cannot be accessed outside of that block.\nif (true) {\n    let blockVar = \"I am a block variable\";\n    console.log(blockVar); // Output: I am a block variable\n}\n// console.log(blockVar); // Uncaught ReferenceError: blockVar is not defined\n\n// =================================================================\n//                              END\n// ================================================================\n\n\n// scope chain : when a variable is accessed, JavaScript looks for it in the current scope. If it doesn't find it, it looks in the parent scope, and so on, until it reaches the global scope. This is known as the scope chain.\nfunction outer() {\n    let outerVar = \"I am an outer variable\";\n    function inner() {\n        console.log(outerVar); // Output: I am an outer variable\n    }\n    inner();\n}\nouter();\n\n// =================================================================\n//                              END\n// ================================================================\n\n\n// closures : a closure is a function that retains access to its outer function's variables even after the outer function has finished executing. This allows the inner function to \"remember\" the environment in which it was created.\nfunction makeCounter() {\n    let count = 0;\n    return function() {\n        count++;\n        console.log(count);\n    }\n}\n\nconst counter = makeCounter();\ncounter(); // Output: 1\ncounter(); // Output: 2\ncounter(); // Output: 3 \n\n// =================================================================\n//                              END\n// ================================================================\n\n\n// lexical scope : a function can access variables from its parent scope, even if the parent function has finished executing. This is because of closures, which allow inner functions to retain access to their outer function's variables.\n\nlet x = 10;\n\nfunction foo() {\n    let x = 20;\n    \n    function bar() {\n        console.log(x); // What does this print?\n    }\n    \n    return bar;\n}\n\nconst baz = foo();\nbaz(); // Output: 20 (because bar lexically scoped to foo)"
  },
