  "ch4-try-catch": {
    "id": "ch4-try-catch",
    "title": "try/catch",
    "minutes": 3,
    "md": "```js\n// catch error\ntry {\n    aler('ok') // alert function name deliberately written wrong\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err.name, err.message) // \"ReferenceError\" \"aler is not defined\"\n}\n```\n\n```js\n// throw keyword\ntry {\n    // Create and throw error\n    throw new Error(\"ok\") // err.msg\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err.name, err.message) // \"error!\" \"ok\" ✅\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n```\n\nthrow error (msg only)\n\n```js\ntry {\n    console.log(new Error(\"error!\")) // Error is an obj with 2 property: name and message\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err) // err logs\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n```\n\nthrow err (name and msg)\n\n```js\ntry {\n    // Create and throw error\n    const error = new Error(\"ok\") // err.msg\n    error.name = \"error!\" // err.name\n    throw error // ✅ This triggers the catch block\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err.name, err.message) // \"error!\" \"ok\" ✅\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n```\n\n```js\ntry {\n  // let stat = true\n  if(stat){\n  console.log('ok') // ok logs\n  }\n  else {\n    console.log(new Error(\"error!\"))\n  }\n}\ncatch(err)  {\n    console.log(err)\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n```\n\n```js\nlet a = 1\n```\n\nWhen the debugger is invoked,\n\nexecution is paused at the debugger statement.\n\nIt is like a breakpoint in the script source.\n\n```js\ndebugger;\nconsole.log(a)\n```\n\nnote: when we use () parenthesis the JS engine reads it in a single line\n\neg:\n\n```js\nlet obj = {\n```\n\nname: 'art',\n\n```js\n    sayHi() { console.log(this.name)}\n}\n```\n\n(obj.sayHi)() // error\n\ncorrect way: add semicolon after obj:\n\n```js\nlet obj = {\n```\n\nname: 'art',\n\n```js\n    sayHi() { console.log(this.name)}\n};\n```\n\n(obj.sayHi)() // art\n",
    "raw": "// catch error\ntry {\n    aler('ok') // alert function name deliberately written wrong\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err.name, err.message) // \"ReferenceError\" \"aler is not defined\"\n}\n\n\n\n\n// throw keyword\ntry {\n    // Create and throw error\n    throw new Error(\"ok\") // err.msg\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err.name, err.message) // \"error!\" \"ok\" ✅\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n\n\n\n\n// throw error (msg only)\ntry {\n    console.log(new Error(\"error!\")) // Error is an obj with 2 property: name and message\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err) // err logs\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n\n\n// throw err (name and msg)\ntry {\n    // Create and throw error\n    const error = new Error(\"ok\") // err.msg\n    error.name = \"error!\" // err.name\n    throw error // ✅ This triggers the catch block\n}\ncatch(err) {\n    console.log(err.name, err.message) // \"error!\" \"ok\" ✅\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n\n\n\n\ntry {\n  // let stat = true\n  if(stat){\n  console.log('ok') // ok logs\n  }\n  else {\n    console.log(new Error(\"error!\"))\n  }\n}\ncatch(err)  {\n    console.log(err)\n}\nfinally {\n    console.log(\"done\")\n}\n\n\n\n\n\nlet a = 1\n// When the debugger is invoked,\n//  execution is paused at the debugger statement.\n//  It is like a breakpoint in the script source.\ndebugger; \nconsole.log(a)\n\n\n\n\n// note: when we use `()` parenthesis the JS engine reads it in a single line\n// eg:\n// let obj = {\n//     name: 'art',\n//     sayHi() { console.log(this.name)}\n// }\n// (obj.sayHi)() // error\n\n\n// correct way: add semicolon after obj:\n\n// let obj = {\n//     name: 'art',\n//     sayHi() { console.log(this.name)}\n// };\n// (obj.sayHi)() // art"
  },
  "ch4-use-strict": {
    "id": "ch4-use-strict",
    "title": "use strict",
    "minutes": 3,
    "md": "```js\n\"use strict\";\n```\n\nit works when used at the top of the file or a function\n\n```js\nit can also work below the comment eg: //comment at line 1 then use 'use strict'; at line 2\n```\n\nit is implictly enabled in classes and modules\n\n#### it does not allows creation of global var\n\n```js\nx = 1\n```\n\nor\n\n```js\nx = { name: \"ok\"}\n```\n\n#### deleting a var\n\n```js\nlet a = 1\ndelete a\n```\n\n#### deleting a function\n\n```js\nfunction x() {}\ndelete x\n```\n\n#### duplicate params name\n\n```js\nfunction x(a,a) {}\n```\n\n#### octal numeric literals are not allowed\n\n```js\nlet x = 010\n```\n\n#### octal escape char are not allowed\n\n```js\nlet x = /010\n```\n\n#### writing to set only property is not allowed\n\n\"use strict\"\n\n```js\nlet obj = {}\nObject.defineProperty(obj , \"name\", {value: \"Saud\", writable: false} )\nobj.name = \"Saad\"\n```\n\n#### creating a variable with var, let inside eval() is not allowed\n\n\"use strict\"\n\n```js\neval(\"x = 1\") // error\neval(\"var y = 1\") // error\neval(\"let z = 1\") // error\neval(\"const w = 1\") // works\n```\n\n#### value of this is the obj called not window inside the function\n\n\"use strict\"\n\n```js\nfunction a(){\n    console.log(this) // undefined not window\n}\n```\n\n#### Keywords reserved for future JavaScript versions can NOT be used as variable names in strict mode.\n\nThese are:\n\nimplements\n\ninterface\n\nlet\n\n```js\n    package\n    private\n```\n\nprotected\n\n```js\n    public\n```\n\nstatic\n\nyield\n\n```js\n\"use strict\";\nlet public = 1500;\n```\n",
    "raw": "\"use strict\";\n\n// it works when used at the top of the file or a function\n// it can also work below the comment eg: //comment at line 1 then use 'use strict'; at line 2\n// it is implictly enabled in classes and modules\n\n//1. it does not allows creation of global var \n// x = 1\n// or \n// x = { name: \"ok\"}\n\n\n// 2. deleting a var\n// let a = 1\n// delete a \n\n// 3. deleting a function\n// function x() {}\n// delete x \n\n// 4. duplicate params name\n// function x(a,a) {}\n\n// 5. octal numeric literals are not allowed\n// let x = 010\n\n// 6. octal escape char are not allowed\n// let x = /010\n\n// 7. writing to set only property is not allowed\n// \"use strict\"\n// let obj = {}\n// Object.defineProperty(obj , \"name\", {value: \"Saud\", writable: false} )\n// obj.name = \"Saad\"\n\n\n// 8. creating a variable with var, let inside eval() is not allowed\n// \"use strict\"\n// eval(\"x = 1\") // error\n// eval(\"var y = 1\") // error\n// eval(\"let z = 1\") // error\n// eval(\"const w = 1\") // works\n\n// 9. value of this is the obj called not window inside the function\n// \"use strict\"\n// function a(){\n//     console.log(this) // undefined not window\n// }\n\n// 10. Keywords reserved for future JavaScript versions can NOT be used as variable names in strict mode.\n\n// These are:\n\n//     implements\n//     interface\n//     let\n//     package\n//     private\n//     protected\n//     public\n//     static\n//     yield\n\n// \"use strict\";\n// let public = 1500;"
  },
